  <li>Dutch (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">nl</code>)</li>
  <li>Brazilian Portuguese (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pt-BR</code>)</li>
  <li>Simplified Chinese (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">zh-Hans</code>)</li>
  <li>Traditional Chinese (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">zh-Hant</code>)</li>
</ul>

<p>If you want a language, <a href="https://brettterpstra.com/contact/">write to me</a> and tell me which folder you would take. I&rsquo;d prefer to keep the work on GitHub, but if you&rsquo;re not familiar with Git and/or GitHub, I&rsquo;m willing to work by shuffling files back and forth. I just lose the audit log in that process.</p>

<p>I don&rsquo;t have a timeline for these additional languages, and I&rsquo;ll publish them when they&rsquo;re marked as confident. The Help is constantly changing and being added to, so I would prefer translators willing to keep up with pull requests in the future.</p>

<p>Previously, I made the mistake of trying to wrangle all of the localizations (and translators) at once and ended up with a mess of half-finished translations. So I&rsquo;ll be tackling these one at a time, with the order determined by who gets back to me first. Germany is my second largest chunk of App Store sales, so I&rsquo;m happy that ended up being the first focus.</p>

<h3 id="finis-support">Finis support</h3>

<p>I&rsquo;ve become somewhat enamored with an app called <a href="https://getfinis.com/">Finis</a>. It&rsquo;s like a lightweight version of Scrivener, focused entirely on Markdown, with a distraction free editor reminiscent of Byword. Fantastic looking, great keyboard shortcuts, index card and outline views, draft/research folders, change/version tracking, and more.</p>

<p>The real beauty of Finis, to me, is that it stores the book as Markdown files, one per chapter or section, with a JSON file and clear schema for assembling the book. Which means Marked can take a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.finis</code> bundle and preview the entire book, in the order you defined in the app. Finis already has some good export options, but this adds a more, plus more styles, and you can see the finished book update as you edit.</p>

<p>In case you missed it, I also improved support for <a href="https://apps.shakingthehabitual.com/traverse/" title="Traverse">Traverse</a> and Tree Outliner. Traverse has especially tight integration, with Streaming Preview support that can differentiate between prose, outline, and Fountain screenwriting. You can open Tree Outliner documents, or embed them in other files as an outline or mind map.</p>

<h3 id="copy-markdown">Copy Markdown</h3>

<p>There is a new <strong>Copy Markdown</strong> command. It is in the Edit menu, the gear menu, and the preview&rsquo;s contextual menu, and the shortcut is <span class="keycombo separated" title="Option-Shift-Command-C"><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8997;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8679;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8984;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="key symbol">C</kbd></span>.</p>

<p>It copies the Markdown Marked used to build the preview. It does not try to turn the rendered HTML back into Markdown. That matters for a page you opened with Markdownify and have not saved yet: the Markdown is only in the preview, and Copy Markdown can still grab it.</p>

<p>In addition, I made all of the special copy commands work on selections, so rather than copying an entire document, you have the option to copy the source/RTF/markdown for a fragment by making a selection first.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Nothing selected: <strong>Copy HTML</strong> (<span class="keycombo separated" title="Shift-Command-C"><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8679;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8984;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="key symbol">C</kbd></span>), <strong>Copy Rich Text</strong> (<span class="keycombo separated" title="Option-Command-C"><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8997;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="mod symbol">&#8984;</kbd><span class="keycombo combiner">+</span><kbd class="key symbol">C</kbd></span>), and <strong>Copy Markdown</strong> each copy the whole document.</li>
  <li>Text selected: each one copies only that selection. Copy HTML keeps the preview&rsquo;s highlighting. Copy Rich Text turns that fragment into rich text. Copy Markdown turns the selection back into Markdown. A code selection comes back as the original source, fence and language included, not as a pile of highlight spans. A selection inside a rendered diagram copies the whole diagram source, such as the Mermaid block, rather than the SVG.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="a-skill-file-for-agents">A skill file for agents</h3>

<p>Help now includes a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">SKILL.md</code> you can hand to an agent. It tells the agent how to open files and folders in Marked, jump to a Settings section, and change preferences through the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">x-marked-3://</code> URL handler. The page, including the skill download, is <a href="https://markedapp.com/help/AI_Integration">AI Integration</a>.</p>

